//! A type applies its family by juxtaposition, as a value call applies its
//! function.
//!
//! The floor spells a value call by juxtaposition, and a type now takes the
//! same spelling. `Couple Integer (Maybe Integer)` is the application
//! `Couple(Integer, Maybe(Integer))`. The call form reads as before, and the
//! two spellings convert.
//!
//! Each argument after the head is one operand, and parentheses group an
//! argument that is an application. The application binds tighter than the
//! returner and the arrow, so `-F Maybe A` returns `Maybe A`. A `(` after a
//! space opens a group, and only a `(` directly after the head continues the
//! call form. A lowercase head stays in the call form, as in
//! `maybeRun(Integer)`. Its juxtaposed form `m T` is a type at a mode
//! variable, which a later unit reads.
//!
//! - see: #5326 — a type reads a juxtaposed application beside its call form
//! - see: #5330 — the juxtaposed type at a mode variable

/// Optional values, `None` and `Some x`.
import "./data-maybe.gandr";

/// This type holds a value of each of two types. Its constructor has the
/// name of the type.
data Couple(a : Type, b : Type) : Type where
  /// A value of each type, the first beside the second.
  | Couple : (first : a, second : b) --> Couple a b

/// An integer beside an optional integer, its type written by juxtaposition.
def coupled() -> Couple Integer (Maybe Integer) =
  Couple 1 (Some 2 : Maybe Integer)

/// The same couple at its type in the call form: the two spellings convert.
def recoupled() -> Couple Integer (Maybe Integer) =
  (Couple 1 (Some 2 : Maybe Integer) : Couple Integer (Maybe Integer))

/// An optional computation's type, named once.
def maybeRun : Type -> Type[-] = fun (A) { -F Maybe A };

/// A present value against the named type at `Integer`.
def presentOne() -> maybeRun(Integer) { Some 1 }
